  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 320 AM EDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nOverall guidance is struggling with the current pattern over the\nnext few days, therefore confidence is not high in the exact\ntiming and placement of rain and thunderstorm chances. However\nsome synoptic features give slightly higher confidence at times.\n\nToday:\n\nWarm air advection will shift into the area this morning along\nwith the low level jet sparking off showers and thunderstorms.\nBest chances (greater than 40 percent) will be north of I-96 with\na 20 to 40 percent chance along and south of I-96. A brief lull in\nshowers and thunderstorms is expected late this morning into the\nearly afternoon. A push of 700mb warm air advection and a weak low\nto mid level front may spark off additional showers and\nthunderstorms this afternoon. Both the HREF and REFS highlight\nthis along and south of I-96 this afternoon. Thunderstorms will be\nelevated with plenty of dry air toward the surface. This could\nresult in some gustier winds with thunderstorms. Some machine\nlearning guidance highlights this potential for stronger winds. \n\nTuesday:\n\nLate tonight into Tuesday additional thunderstorms are possible\nwith the best chances during the day (30 percent) with the low \nlevel jet moving over the area and positive vorticity advection. \nMean MUCAPE values are around 2000 J/kg with mean sfc-500mb shear \nvalues around 30 to 40 knots. Differences within the various \nsolutions bring lower confidence to Tuesday's forecast. If storms\ndevelop there is a marginal risk for them to be severe with winds\nas the main concern. Tuesday will also be one of the hottest days\nthis week with highs in the mid 80s to low 90s and heat index \nvalues in the 90s with the potential for values to approach 100 in\nsouthern Michigan.\n\nTuesday night the warm front lifts north with areas along and\nnorth of I-96 seeing an additional chance for thunderstorms.\n\n\nRest of Week:\n\nWednesday will be another hot day with heat index values in the\n90s mainly along and south of a Grand Haven to Mount Pleasant\nline. Areas along and south of I-94 could see heat index values\naround 100. This will be highly dependent on thunderstorm chances.\nSome solutions have a weak shortwave moving through during the\nafternoon and evening and others have a stronger trough and low\npressure system along with a stronger jet. If this latter solution\npans out there will be a higher risk for severe thunderstorms.\nStay tuned.\n\nNorthwest flow returns to the area Thursday into the weekend.\nWe'll be on the northeast side of the ridge parked over the\ncentral CONUS. Within this flow we'll need to watch for ridge\nriders bringing additional rain and thunderstorm chances. High\ntemperatures will be cooler in this flow (70s and 80s), but dew\npoints will largely be in the 60s.",
